CoroutineContext is a collection of information used by a coroutine. It can contain elements such as Job, CoroutineName, CoroutineDispatcher and CoroutineExceptionHandler.Its like a map/set of coroutine information. Each element has a unique Key used to find it. 
Each context element is also a CoroutineContext.
Eg.
val name = CoroutineName("MyCoroutine")
val context: CoroutineContext = name
Every element has a unique key. Their keys allow us to retrieve them from the context. 

To get an element from Context we use [] or get().
Eg. using []
val ctx: CoroutineContext = CoroutineName("MyCoroutine")
val name = ctx[CoroutineName]
println(name?.name) \\MyCoroutine
Eg.Using get():
ctx.get(CoroutineName)
Both are equivalent. If the element does not exist, null is returned.Context[Key] returns the elemmen att the key
Contexts can be added using +.
Eg.
val ctx1 = CoroutineName("Name1")
val ctx2 = Job()
val ctx3 = ctx1 + ctx2
Now ctx3 contains both:
ctx3[CoroutineName]
ctx3[Job]
If two elements have the same key, the new element replaces the old one.
Eg.
val ctx1 = CoroutineName("Name1")
val ctx2 = CoroutineName("Name2")
val ctx3 = ctx1 + ctx2
println(ctx3[CoroutineName]?.name) \\Name2
If different key then both are kept. If same key then new one replaces old one
An empty context can also be used which contains no elements.
eg.
val empty: CoroutineContext = EmptyCoroutineContext
println(empty[CoroutineName]) // null
println(empty[Job])           // null

Adding an empty context changes nothing.
eg.
val ctx = empty + CoroutineName("Name1") + empty
We can remmove an elemment usign minusKey().
Eg.
val ctx = CoroutineName("Name1") + Job()
val ctx2 = ctx.minusKey(CoroutineName)
Now:
ctx2[CoroutineName] // null
ctx2[Job]           // Job
Syntax:
context.minusKey(Key)
fold() can process every element in the context like in Collections. It takes an initial value and then performd. operation using the accumulator and current element.
Eg.
val ctx = CoroutineName("Name1") + Job()
ctx.fold("") { acc, element ->
    "$acc$element "
}.also(::println)
So, fold processes every element and build one result
A child coroutine inherits the context of its parent by default.
Eg.
fun main() = runBlocking(CoroutineName("main")) {
    launch {
        println(coroutineContext[CoroutineName]?.name)
    }
}
The child inherits main.Child Can Override Parent Context. A child can provide its own context.
Eg.
fun main() = runBlocking(CoroutineName("main")) {
    launch(CoroutineName("child")) {
        println(coroutineContext[CoroutineName]?.name) \\child
    }
}
The child's element replaces the parent's element when they have the same key.Same-key child elements override parent elements. 
A normal CoroutineScope can access coroutineContext. A suspend function can also access the context through coroutineContext.
Eg.
suspend fun main() = withContext(CoroutineName("Outer")) {
    printName() \\Outer
    launch(CoroutineName("Inner")) {
        printName() \\Inner
    }
}
Inside a suspend function coroutineContext lets you access the current coroutine's context.
We can also create our own CoroutineContext.Element.
Basic structure:
class MyCustomContext : CoroutineContext.Element {
    override val key: CoroutineContext.Key<> = Key
    companion object Key :
        CoroutineContext.Key<MyCustomContext>
}
The Key identifies the context element. 
Eg.
class CounterContext(
    private val name: String
) : CoroutineContext.Element {
    override val key: CoroutineContext.Key<> = Key
    private var nextNumber = 0
    fun printNext() {
        println("$name: $nextNumber")
        nextNumber++
    }
    companion object Key :
        CoroutineContext.Key<CounterContext>
}
We can also access it from a suspend function.
Eg.
suspend fun printNext() {
    coroutineContext[CounterContext]?.printNext()
}
The context is inherited by child coroutines, and a child can replace it with another CounterContext. 
The book also shows custom contexts being used to provide different implementations for production and testing.
Like for Production, RealUuidProviderContext is provided and for Testing is provided FakeUuidProviderContext